Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: preserve the previous global boost state on error

The global boost store intentionally retries a request for the current state
so all policies can be synchronized. If no policy supports boost, or a policy
callback rejects that retry, restoring !state changes the global flag despite
the rejected write. Remember its actual previous value under the driver lock.

Observed on Uke with three policies without boost OPPs: a rejected write of
zero changed the global flag from zero to one. This does not add boost OPPs,
change policy capabilities or make the per-policy update loop transactional.

Base: Linux v7.3.1, 79a907609d53d5cd645b42c11550d295d43abf99.
Original cpufreq core license and attribution are retained.

--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq.c
@@ -2814,6 +2814,7 @@ static int cpufreq_boost_trigger_state(int state)
 {
 	struct cpufreq_policy *policy;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	bool previous_state;
 	int ret = -EOPNOTSUPP;
 
 	/*
@@ -2822,6 +2823,7 @@ static int cpufreq_boost_trigger_state(int state)
 	 */
 
 	write_lock_irqsave(&cpufreq_driver_lock, flags);
+	previous_state = cpufreq_driver->boost_enabled;
 	cpufreq_driver->boost_enabled = state;
 	write_unlock_irqrestore(&cpufreq_driver_lock, flags);
 
@@ -2843,7 +2845,7 @@ static int cpufreq_boost_trigger_state(int state)
 		return 0;
 
 	write_lock_irqsave(&cpufreq_driver_lock, flags);
-	cpufreq_driver->boost_enabled = !state;
+	cpufreq_driver->boost_enabled = previous_state;
 	write_unlock_irqrestore(&cpufreq_driver_lock, flags);
 
 	pr_err("%s: Cannot %s BOOST\n",
